{"success": true, "name": "arc-compact-panel-encoding", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md", "content": "# Session learning: lattice-like compact outputs

The test was a 24x28 repeating 3x3 panel field separated by color-9 bands, with distinctive colored values at selected separator intersections. Two demos confirmed that this general puzzle class can produce compact outputs, but neither established a test mapping: demo 1 was 23x29 -> 5x4, demo 2 was 26x29 -> 3x4, while the test was 24x28. Their compact outputs preserve some colors from the separator markings and include the filler/background value (9 in demo 1, 7 in demo 2). Dimensions do not match a simple count of all intersections or panels. The tested agent requested a third demo without stating a concrete pair of competing mappings or the predicted observation that would distinguish them; no verified output rule was obtained.

Reusable diagnostic: before requesting another example, lay out candidate coordinate maps and output conventions. For each demo, record exact positions/values of exceptional separator cells and compact output. Test possible selection of intersections, row/column reduction, edge inclusion, ordering, and background/default encoding against every observed pair. Make a further request only if the competing hypotheses yield a specific different predicted outcome, then immediately use the returned example to resolve that difference. Do not infer the test result from visual lattice resemblance or output shape alone.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u078/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-compact-panel-encoding/references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/panel-count-encoding.md", "content": "# Panel marker-count encoding: diagnostic and validation lesson

This instance had grids partitioned by full separator bands and compact outputs shaped like the panel array. Several demos were consistent with an output marker for panels containing multiple instances of the designated non-background color; others had different marker colors and output backgrounds. A test answer based on a guessed threshold and manually counted panels was rejected, so the evidence does not establish the exact test encoding or make a reusable color/threshold rule.

Reusable method: transcribe the grid exactly; locate separator rows/columns; define half-open panel bounds; count target-color cells in every panel; build a table of panel coordinates, counts, and corresponding output values. Test competing count mappings (including exact count versus threshold) against *all* demonstrations, especially panels with zero, one, two, and three-or-more markers. Confirm orientation, output dimensions, background encoding, and values separately. After rejection, first independently re-check serialization and the panel count table; do not repeat a merely reformatted candidate. Do not claim a count rule until full demo replay supports it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u078/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/panel-count-encoding.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-compact-panel-encoding", "file": "references/failed-output-grid-recovery.md", "content": "# Compact-output recovery after rejection

This task demonstrated a costly failure pattern: after a compact panel task's answer was rejected, subsequent answers repeated the same sparse marker interpretation, including duplicate grids, while one attempt accidentally had an invalid row width. Treat each negative result as falsifying the whole candidate rule. Log candidate grids/hypotheses, then derive the compact output dimensions and cell mapping from demos before submitting again. Validate dimensions and row widths separately from the transformation. Do not repeat a candidate or vary only its serialization; a new submission needs a materially different mapping that replays every available demo.
