{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-region-mapping.md", "content": "# Compact region-to-output mapping

When a partitioned input has a much smaller output, investigate a region summary as a live task class instead of assuming a same-size edit or stamp. Identify separator rows/columns, compute the number and arrangement of regions, and associate each region with the corresponding output cell. Inspect marker presence, marker color/identity, count, and position within each region as candidate encodings. Verify the exact output—including default values for empty regions—against every demonstration before transfer. Output dimensions alone do not establish the mapping. Before spending another demo request, list at least two candidate encodings and state what observation in the next pair would distinguish them.

In a demo with sparse markers embedded in a regular partitioned canvas, the compact output preserved a 6×3 region arrangement and represented localized marker colors at corresponding output positions; most empty positions retained the canvas color. Treat this as a diagnostic pattern, not a universal rule: confirm separators and correspondence in the current task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u078/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-region-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-stream-solving", "file": "references/output-dimension-pitfall.md", "content": "# Compact outputs are evidence

Before inferring an ARC transformation, compare input and demonstration-output dimensions. A smaller output is a strong signal that the task may extract or encode a property, rather than paint a full-size grid. Do not assume a template-stamping task solely because the input contains separators, repeating blocks, or sparse colored markers.

Derive the output structure from the examples: identify repeated tiles/regions, inspect how the sparse markers relate spatially to each region, and map the demonstrated selection or encoding to the compact output. Validate every output cell against the inputs. If multiple mappings still fit, request a demo chosen to distinguish those hypotheses; never submit an arbitrary placeholder based on an unsupported visual guess.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u078/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/continual-arc-stream-solving/references/output-dimension-pitfall.md"}
